WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:06.025
Two functions on the integers.

2
00:00:06.025 --> 00:00:07.274
f adds one.

3
00:00:07.274 --> 00:00:08.420
g doubles.

4
00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:10.308
Start at four.

5
00:00:10.418 --> 00:00:13.275
Is g of f of four the same

6
00:00:13.385 --> 00:00:15.363
as f of g of four?

7
00:00:15.363 --> 00:00:17.890
Pause and work it out.

8
00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:25.747
g of f of four means: first f, then g.

9
00:00:25.747 --> 00:00:28.336
Four plus one is five

10
00:00:28.336 --> 00:00:31.150
and five doubled is ten.

11
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:35.100
f of g of four: first double, eight

12
00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.100
then add one, nine.

13
00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:40.700
Ten is not nine: the order matters.

14
00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:45.389
So read g circle f from right to left

15
00:00:45.389 --> 00:00:47.330
f first, then g.

16
00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:49.863
Back to the seating plan P.

17
00:00:49.863 --> 00:00:51.816
Now the seats sit in rows

18
00:00:51.816 --> 00:00:54.637
seats one and two at the front, three

19
00:00:54.637 --> 00:00:56.590
four and five at the back.

20
00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:57.770
Follow Ana

21
00:00:57.770 --> 00:01:00.485
her arrow lands on seat number two

22
00:01:00.485 --> 00:01:03.124
and that seat is in the front row.

23
00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:05.010
So the composed function

24
00:01:05.085 --> 00:01:06.820
sends Ana to the front.

25
00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:10.395
The composition skips the middle set

26
00:01:10.476 --> 00:01:12.662
one arrow from each student

27
00:01:12.743 --> 00:01:14.200
straight to a row.

28
00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:18.167
If f and g are both one-to-one

29
00:01:18.167 --> 00:01:20.268
so is g circle f.

30
00:01:20.384 --> 00:01:23.418
Suppose g of f of a equals

31
00:01:23.535 --> 00:01:25.635
g of f of a prime.

32
00:01:25.635 --> 00:01:27.619
g is one-to-one

33
00:01:27.619 --> 00:01:31.236
so f of a equals f of a prime.

34
00:01:31.236 --> 00:01:35.670
f is one-to-one, so a equals a prime.

35
00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:38.990
Our r circle P is not one-to-one

36
00:01:38.990 --> 00:01:42.050
Ana and Cai both reach the front

37
00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:45.740
because P already puts them in seat two.

38
00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:49.307
Now five students in five seats

39
00:01:49.307 --> 00:01:52.013
no seat shared: a bijection.

40
00:01:52.013 --> 00:01:54.253
Turn every arrow round.

41
00:01:54.347 --> 00:01:57.240
Each seat has exactly one arrow

42
00:01:57.333 --> 00:01:59.200
back to its student.

43
00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:03.019
That is the inverse function, f inverse.

44
00:02:03.106 --> 00:02:06.402
f inverse after f brings every student

45
00:02:06.488 --> 00:02:08.830
back to where they started.

46
00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:12.357
Try it with the first plan, P.

47
00:02:12.357 --> 00:02:14.617
Turn its arrows round.

48
00:02:14.617 --> 00:02:17.465
Seat two now has two arrows

49
00:02:17.465 --> 00:02:19.332
to Ana and to Cai

50
00:02:19.332 --> 00:02:22.770
and seats one and three have none.

51
00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:25.116
That is not a function.

52
00:02:25.116 --> 00:02:28.760
Only a bijection has an inverse function.

53
00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:31.909
For h of x equals three x plus

54
00:02:31.994 --> 00:02:33.792
two on the rationals

55
00:02:33.792 --> 00:02:36.618
undo the steps in reverse order

56
00:02:36.618 --> 00:02:39.700
subtract two, then divide by three.

57
00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:43.007
So h inverse of five is one

58
00:02:43.007 --> 00:02:45.752
because h of one is five.

59
00:02:45.752 --> 00:02:48.920
It is not one over h of five

60
00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:51.666
which is one seventeenth.

61
00:02:51.666 --> 00:02:55.890
The minus one means undo, not one over.

62
00:02:56.470 --> 00:02:58.957
Seven students, six seats.

63
00:02:58.957 --> 00:03:02.209
Seat the first six, one per seat.

64
00:03:02.209 --> 00:03:05.557
The seventh has nowhere new to go

65
00:03:05.557 --> 00:03:07.470
some seat gets two.

66
00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:10.806
That is the pigeonhole principle.

67
00:03:10.896 --> 00:03:14.334
A function from a bigger finite set to

68
00:03:14.424 --> 00:03:17.500
a smaller one is never one-to-one.

69
00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:20.390
More objects say more.

70
00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:24.170
Thirty trays of bread, seven ovens.

71
00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:28.790
Some oven bakes at least thirty over seven

72
00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:30.680
rounded up: five.

73
00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:34.853
Two fives and five fours make thirty

74
00:03:34.957 --> 00:03:37.333
so five is the most the

75
00:03:37.437 --> 00:03:39.710
principle can promise.

76
00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:43.988
But it says some oven, not every oven.

77
00:03:43.988 --> 00:03:48.020
Here five of the ovens bake only four.

78
00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:50.873
Now a fact about numbers.

79
00:03:50.873 --> 00:03:52.965
Take any six integers

80
00:03:53.056 --> 00:03:55.965
and sort them by their remainder

81
00:03:56.056 --> 00:03:57.875
on division by five.

82
00:03:57.875 --> 00:04:01.512
Seventeen and forty-two both leave two.

83
00:04:01.512 --> 00:04:05.240
Eight and thirty-three both leave three.

84
00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:08.144
Six numbers, five remainders

85
00:04:08.144 --> 00:04:10.082
two must share a box

86
00:04:10.082 --> 00:04:13.957
and their difference is a multiple of five.

87
00:04:13.957 --> 00:04:16.070
Here it is twenty-five.

88
00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:18.386
For infinite sets

89
00:04:18.386 --> 00:04:22.051
same size means there is a bijection.

90
00:04:22.051 --> 00:04:26.006
List the integers: zero, minus one, one

91
00:04:26.006 --> 00:04:28.610
minus two, two, and so on.

92
00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:31.834
Every integer appears exactly once

93
00:04:31.916 --> 00:04:34.379
so the natural numbers and the

94
00:04:34.461 --> 00:04:36.760
integers have the same size.

95
00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:39.099
But no set has the same

96
00:04:39.185 --> 00:04:41.087
size as its power set.

97
00:04:41.087 --> 00:04:43.335
That is Cantor's theorem

98
00:04:43.335 --> 00:04:45.150
proved in the notes.

99
00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:50.454
To sum up: g circle f means f first, then g.

100
00:04:50.454 --> 00:04:53.998
Only a bijection has an inverse.

101
00:04:54.105 --> 00:04:56.682
And p objects in h boxes

102
00:04:56.789 --> 00:05:00.440
put at least p over h, rounded up

103
00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:02.050
into some box.

104
00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:05.651
Try it: a playlist has fifty songs

105
00:05:05.746 --> 00:05:07.467
by twelve artists.

106
00:05:07.563 --> 00:05:09.953
Show that some artist has

107
00:05:10.048 --> 00:05:11.960
at least five songs.
